<<<
SIMILE & METAPHOR, allegory & parable. Allegory (uttering things otherwise) & parable (putting side by side) are almost exchangeable terms.
===
**SIMILE & METAPHOR,** *allegory & parable.* *Allegory* (uttering things otherwise) & *parable* (putting side by side) are almost exchangeable terms.
>>>

<<<
Usage, however, has decided that parable is the fitter name for the illustrative story designed to answer a single question or suggest a single principle, & offering a definite moral, while allegory is to be preferred when the application is less restricted,
===
Usage, however, has decided that *parable* is the fitter name for the illustrative story designed to answer a single question or suggest a single principle, & offering a definite moral, while *allegory* is to be preferred when the application is less restricted,
>>>

<<<
the most important factor being the use of parable to denote the allegorical stories told by Christ.
===
the most important factor being the use of *parable* to denote the allegorical stories told by Christ.
>>>
